      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\), we start by identifying the general form of an integer that satisfies this condition. Such an integer can be expressed as:\n\\[ x = 22k + 15 \\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. For \\(x\\) to be a 4-digit number, it must satisfy:\n\\[ 1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\n\nFirst, we solve the upper bound inequality:\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\leq 9999 \\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[ 22k \\leq 9984 \\]\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\\[ k \\leq \\frac{9984}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\leq 453 \\]\n\nNext, we solve the lower bound inequality:\n\\[ 22k + 15 \\geq 1000 \\]\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\\[ 22k \\geq 985 \\]\nDivide both sides by 22:\n\\[ k \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\]\n\\[ k \\geq 44.7727 \\]\nSince \\(k\\) must be an integer, we round up to the next whole number:\n\\[ k \\geq 45 \\]\n\nCombining the results from both inequalities, we find that \\(k\\) must be an integer between 45 and 453 inclusive. To find the largest 4-digit integer in this range, we choose the greatest integer \\(k\\) that is an exact fit for the inequality:\n\\[ k = 453 - 1 = 452 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(k = 452\\) back into the expression for \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = 22 \\times 452 + 15 \\]\nCalculate the product:\n\\[ 22 \\times 452 = 9944 \\]\nAdd 15 to the product:\n\\[ x = 9944 + 15 = 9959 \\]\n\nFinally, we verify that 9959 is a 4-digit integer congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\):\n\\[ 9959 - 15 = 9944 \\]\n\\[ 9944 \\div 22 = 453 \\text{ with no remainder} \\]\nSince 9944 is exactly divisible by 22, we have:\n\\[ 9959 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22} \\]\n\nThus, the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\(15 \\pmod{22}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9959}\n\\]",
